```

**Why it matters:** a change to the gate classifier silently changes the battery's membership, and nothing in the retained evidence says which version of that classifier made the decision. `docs/core/governance.md` requires every count and claim to be re-measurable from the published tree; this decision is not.

### F-2 — Medium — `tools/test-gates.mjs check` can never fail; the guard its own header advertises is inert

**Where:** `tools/test-gates.mjs:102-105` (`classify` falls back to `DEFAULT_GATE`), `tools/test-gates.mjs:129-144` (the `check` command), advertised at `tools/test-gates.mjs:18-19`.

`classify()` returns one of the five known gate ids or `'correctness'`. The check's condition is `!known.has(gate) && gate !== DEFAULT_GATE`, which is therefore never true for any input. The file's header states: *"check is wired into the correctness gate, so adding an unclassified test fails the suite instead of silently joining the constant path."* It does not — an unclassified test silently joins `correctness`.

**Reproduction** — `r-b-inventory.mjs` section B4:

```
   classify("src/whatever.test.ts") = "correctness" -> check would NOT FLAG
   classify("tests/unclassified.test.ts") = "correctness" -> check would NOT FLAG
   classify("zzz/never-seen.test.ts") = "correctness" -> check would NOT FLAG
   classify("benchmarks/x.test.ts") = "benchmarks" -> check would NOT FLAG
"check" can only flag a file whose gate id is neither a known gate nor 'correctness': UNREACHABLE - the check can never fail
```

**Why it matters:** this is a documented guard in the checker's own dependency closure, and `npm test` is `node tools/test-gates.mjs run correctness` — the constant path is what CI actually runs.

### F-3 — Medium — the recorded working-tree state never gates the verdict, and the retained evidence was produced on a dirty tree

**Where:** `scripts/release-check.mjs:175-176` writes `report.sourceCommit` and `report.sourceStatus`; neither is read anywhere else (`sourceStatus` occurs exactly once in the file — the write). The battery runs regardless at `scripts/release-check.mjs:188`.

The checker records the commit and the dirty-tree state, then never consults them. A battery can report *"Local battery passed"* on a tree whose committed source differs from the executed source.

This is not hypothetical — the author's own retained artifact shows it happened. `docs/reports/release-battery-evidence-2026-10-30.admission.json` records:

```
sourceCommit: 0b393d84ae1bdb14b1d5ee8d6e22a8cf64f86b8b
sourceStatus: " M scripts/release-check.mjs\n M vitest.config.ts\n?? docs/decisions/0015-release-battery-evidence.md\n?? scripts/release-check-evidence.mjs\n?? tests/tools/release-check-evidence.test.mjs\n?? tests/tools/release-check-runner.test.mjs\n"
```

That run happened with **the checker itself modified** and **`scripts/release-check-evidence.mjs` untracked**, and with `vitest.config.ts` modified — the very base config the generated Vitest config merges. The natural explanation is that the repair was still in progress; the defect is that the checker recorded this and did not act on it, and the evidence report `docs/reports/release-battery-evidence-2026-10-30.md` does not mention it. A reader of that report cannot tell the run happened on a modified tree.

**Reproduction** — `r-b-inventory.mjs` section B6:

```
occurrences of "sourceStatus" in scripts/release-check.mjs: 1
   report.sourceStatus=execFileSync('git',['-C',root,'status','--porcelain'],{encoding:'utf8',windowsHide:true});
```

**Why it matters:** "Local battery passed" currently does not mean "this commit passed". Given `AGENTS.md`'s publication gate, a dirty tree should be an explicit admission failure rather than an unremarked field.

### F-4 — Low/Medium — three machine-report guards and one file-status branch are inert against the installed reporter (vitest 5.0.1)

**Where:** `scripts/release-check-evidence.mjs:36-48` (`numRuntimeErrorTestSuites`, `wasInterrupted`, `openHandles`) and `scripts/release-check-evidence.mjs:56` (`file.status === 'pending' || file.status === 'skipped'`).

The installed reporter emits exactly these keys (`node_modules/vitest/dist/chunks/index.DzobfTyw.js:17864-17879`): `numTotalTestSuites, numPassedTestSuites, numFailedTestSuites, numPendingTestSuites, numTotalTests, numPassedTests, numFailedTests, numPendingTests, numTodoTests, snapshot, startTime, success, testResults, coverageMap`. It never emits `wasInterrupted`, `numRuntimeErrorTestSuites` or `openHandles`, and `testResults[].status` is only ever `'failed'` or `'passed'` (line 17859).

**Reproduction** — every one of the seven real-reporter cases in `r-d-runner-reality.mjs` printed:

```
REAL reporter has fields  : wasInterrupted=false numRuntimeErrorTestSuites=false openHandles=false
```

(Positive control: `numTotalTests` *is* present in the same file — the grep was re-run with a narrow path after a first broad grep returned a false negative caused by dependency-directory ignore rules.)

**Why it matters:** the evidence report claims *"Failed, skipped, pending, interrupted or incomplete required evidence cannot pass."* Interruption is in practice caught only by the non-zero process exit, and runtime-error suites only by the suite counters — the three named machine checks never execute. **No false pass results** (skips are still caught by the assertion counters, verified below), but the documented evidence model overstates the machine checks that exist.

### F-5 — Low — `executePlan` requires the caller to pre-create its output directory

**Where:** `scripts/release-check.mjs:102` (`jsonFile` with `flag:'wx'`), `:126`, `:137`.

The exported `executePlan` writes `node-files.json` / `vitest.config.mjs` into `out` without creating it and throws `ENOENT` if `out` is absent. The CLI path is safe — `runReleaseCheck` does `mkdirSync(out,{recursive:true})` at `scripts/release-check.mjs:164` — but the exported API's contract is not self-sufficient. My harness hit this twice with raw `ENOENT` before creating the directory. Latent, not a CLI defect.

### F-6 — Low — the three inventory sources use three different file-name grammars

**Where:** `scripts/release-check.mjs:67` accepts `.cts`/`.jsx`; `tools/node-test-runners.mjs:20` and `tools/test-gates.mjs:81` do not.

`testPlan` accepts `(?:ts|mts|cts|tsx|js|mjs|cjs|jsx)`; both gate-side inventory functions accept `(?:ts|mts|js|mjs|cjs)`. On this tree the three agree exactly — the only difference is the 15 intentional benchmark exclusions (B2: 0 files selected by the checker but absent from `listTestFiles`; 0 node files selected by the checker but absent from `nodeTestFiles`). A future `.test.cts` file would be selected by the checker and invisible to both gate tools, so it would escape the gate accounting entirely.

